   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-ab",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.14",
    "topic_title": "Connecting Infinite Limits and Vertical Asymptotes",
    "stem": "Let f(x) = 3/(x−2) for x ≠ 2. Which statement correctly describes the behavior of f near x = 2 and its connection to a vertical asymptote?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "lim x→2⁻ f(x) = ∞ and lim x→2⁺ f(x) = −∞; the line x = 2 is a vertical asymptote.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Reversed-side sign error: this assigns a positive denominator to the left side and a negative denominator to the right side. In fact, x−2 is negative when x < 2 and positive when x > 2. Fix: Test the denominator’s sign at one input on each side of 2."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "lim x→2⁻ f(x) = ∞ and lim x→2⁺ f(x) = ∞; the line x = 2 is a vertical asymptote.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Ignoring the denominator’s sign: a denominator approaching zero does not always produce positive unbounded values. On the left, the positive numerator divided by a negative denominator gives negative values with increasing magnitude. Fix: Determine the quotient’s sign on each side before assigning ∞ or −∞."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "lim x→2⁻ f(x) = −∞ and lim x→2⁺ f(x) = ∞; the line x = 2 is a vertical asymptote.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "From the left, x−2 is negative and approaches zero, so f(x) decreases without bound. From the right, x−2 is positive and approaches zero, so f(x) increases without bound. This unbounded behavior makes x = 2 a vertical asymptote."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The two-sided limit as x→2 does not exist, so x = 2 is not a vertical asymptote.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Two-sided-limit requirement error: a vertical asymptote does not require matching one-sided limits. Although the two-sided limit does not exist here, the one-sided unbounded behavior establishes the vertical asymptote. Fix: Check each one-sided limit for unbounded behavior before ruling out a vertical asymptote."
     }
    ]
   },
